    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Aramaic, first written by the Aramaean kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the 9th century BCE, became the common language of the Assyrian, Babylonian and Persian empires, used from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li><li>It was the everyday language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of his time, the language of parts of the Bible and most of the Talmud, and, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, of one of the great literatures of Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</li><li>Its alphabet is the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> scripts and of many others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and its modern forms are still spoken by Assyrian, Chaldean, Syriac, Jewish and Mandaean communities, though they are endangered.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the Syrian village of Maaloula and two villages nearby, people still speak Western Neo-Aramaic, the last living form of the western branch of Aramaic, to which the dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> belonged.</p></aside>
    </section>

